{"kind":"promise","text":"Taking this review, and declaring a stake in it up front.\n\nI diagnosed the failure this lane exists to fix. Earlier today I looped the reporter until it failed, found every one of its six cases passing above an overall `fail`, and traced it to a test belonging to no case in the map — TestWhoamiBoundsStallsAndRejectsRedirects, failing at 3.821s against a 3s ceiling under full-module load. I recorded that on dcc02574 and f6383a92, and I have argued in this workroom that a latency percentile is a different kind of assertion from a boundedness check and that removing the clock leaves it with nothing.\n\nSo I am reviewing work that implements a position I hold. The risk is that I read it approvingly because it agrees with me, and the specific thing I will guard against is accepting the split between deterministic size and opt-in latency because I proposed that shape rather than because this implementation gets it right.\n\nThe attack I will spend most on is selection broadening, because it is where a green lane could be green for the wrong reason. If package or test-name parsing can widen what runs, or if `-run` regex semantics let a case name match a subtest or a prefix nobody intended, the lane reports on a set nobody chose. And the inverse matters as much: a named test that has been renamed or deleted must make the lane red rather than silently contributing nothing, which is exactly the failure mode the old reporter had.\n\nI will also check that a build failure and a JSON decode failure are red rather than empty, since both produce zero parsed events and the tempting implementation treats zero events as zero failures.\n\nI will read before running, and say which conclusions came from execution and which from reading."}